This course adheres to a student-centered teaching philosophy, aiming to help students understand the application of AI technology in the field of national governance. It focuses on mastering the core concepts, fundamental theories, practical cases, and innovative frontiers of AI technology in empowering national governance, thereby fostering a comprehensive and clear understanding of the proposition "Artificial Intelligence and National Governance." The course employs peer instruction methods, encouraging students to engage in autonomous discussions, raise questions in class, and reach consensus to deepen their understanding. Additionally, students will be required to undertake group-based independent learning and practical internships, collaborating on research projects to apply the theoretical knowledge they have acquired to research questions of their interest. The course content will primarily cover the following six areas:
(1) Exploring digital political phenomena;
(2) Exploring political phenomena related to emerging media;
(3) Exploring digital governance and government innovation;
(4) Exploring public participation in the digital age;
(5) Exploring national capabilities in the digital age;
(6) The application of large-scale language models in national governance.

This course is a collaborative offering within the Department of International Politics and the Department of Diplomacy. Each week, one faculty member will deliver a lecture introducing, analyzing, and elaborating on the following topics:  
1. The current landscape of international relations and hotspot issues;  
2. The foreign policies of major world powers today;  
3. China’s current foreign relations and foreign policy.

This course will help students master:
The system of diplomacy, including the nature of diplomacy，historical evolution, norms and principles, organization and personnel systems of diplomacy;
The content and process of bilateral diplomacy: including the basic functions of bilateral diplomacy, diplomatic negotiations, diplomatic agreements and international treaties;
The development and trend of modern diplomacy, including summit diplomacy, multilateral diplomacy, mediation diplomacy, public diplomacy, and overall diplomacy;
The characteristics, style, and development of Chinese diplomacy;
Students can have a systematic understanding of the role of diplomacy in contemporary international relations and master basic knowledge and skills for diplomacy.

Political Anthropology is a general education elective course offered to undergraduate students at the School of International Relations and Public Affairs, Fudan University. This course introduces the fundamental theoretical frameworks and methodological approaches of political anthropology, examining political systems, phenomena, and behavior through the lens of social systems. The course is divided into six main units:
Methodologies in Political Anthropology;
The Origins of Power and Politics;
The Evolution of Political Institutions and the State;
Techniques of Power in the Political Realm;
The World after Modernization;
Domination and Resistance.

Students are expected to leave the course with:
Appreciation of major theoretical perspectives as well as analytical tools in political theories in general, and comparative political theories in particular;
knowledge of political cultures, their political roots and the long shadows they usually cast on national politics;
Exposure to some comparative strategies at theoretical as well as empirical level.

This course is a foundational course in public international law with international relations characteristics designed for non-law student. The curriculum includes:History of International Law; Fundamental Principles of International Law;China's Relationship with International Law.
[Learning Requirements]
(1) Master the fundamental ethos and principles of international law;
(2) Examine China's participation and practical approaches in various fields of international law;
(3) Explore issues of international law and institutional system construction within the context of China's rising global influence;
(4) Integrate the study of international law with international relations research.

